  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Parc naturel départemental du Vinaigrier sits on the heights above Nice, between the Riquier district and the Col de Villefranche, following an old water-supply channel converted into a walking trail. The route climbs gently to around 370 metres of altitude, offering views over the city, the surrounding hills and the sea, in a setting of pines and scrubland that feels much quieter than the seafront.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The trail has no climb because it follows the old channel that brought water from the hills to Nice. It is easy and suitable for the whole family: 2 km according to the tourist office, about 1h15 for the Département's route, which rates it medium difficulty. The regulation also bans animals from the landscaped areas (play areas, educational areas, fitness areas, sports circuits) and bikes, and requires dog waste to be picked up. The park has a 50-space car park at 315 boulevard de l'Observatoire; by bus, line 84 stops at "Observatoire" (300 m on foot via avenue Brès). The park's regulation, published by the Alpes-Maritimes Département, requires dogs to wear a collar and stay on a leash year-round; category-1 dogs are banned and category-2 dogs must be muzzled. The park is open 7:30am-8pm from 1 April to 31 October and 8am-6pm from 1 November to 31 March. The Département specifies that some trail sections are closed to the public: follow the signs. Access to the trail is via the Chemin du Vinaigrier, followed by a few steps. The terrain is a limestone plateau covered in old farming terraces ("restanques"), planted with Aleppo pines and holm oaks that give shade, mostly in the morning or late afternoon in summer, when the sun is lower. Bring water for your dog: we found no official water point along the route.</p>
      <p><strong>Worth checking before you go:</strong> opening hours, which the Département can change, and closed trail sections (signs); the tourist office gives 2 km, the Département 1h15 for the route. Sources read on 06/11/2026: park regulation (order of 17/12/2019), pages of the Département and Randoxygène, Explorateurs des Alpes-Maritimes, Côte d'Azur France, CRT Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: a good option for a short, shaded walk with your dog without leaving Nice, especially in the late afternoon once the heat eases off.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Mont Boron and its park, reachable on foot or by car, let you extend the outing with another view over the Baie des Anges and Nice's harbour.</p>
